<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Napoléon Louis Bonaparte</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1831">1831</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Louis II of Holland</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Napoléon Louis Bonaparte was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a>, King of Holland, and Hortense de Beauharnais, and the elder brother of the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>. Made Grand Duke of Berg as a small child in 1809, he was briefly proclaimed King of Holland as Louis II when his father abdicated in 1810. He died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> in 1831 after joining a rising against the Papal States.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/napoleon-louis-bonaparte.jpg" alt="Napoléon Louis Bonaparte" width="320" height="414" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thesupermat · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Napol%C3%A9on_III_et_l%27Italie_-_F%C3%A9lix_Cottrau_-_Le_prince_Napol%C3%A9on-Louis_Bonaparte_-_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Napoléon Louis Bonaparte was born on 11 October 1804 and was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a>, who ruled the Napoleonic Kingdom of Holland from 1806 to 1810 as King Louis I. His father was the younger brother of Napoleon I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His mother was Queen Hortense, daughter of Josephine de Beauharnais, Napoleon's first wife. His younger brother, Louis-Napoléon, became Emperor of the French in 1852 as Napoléon III.</p><p>His elder brother, Napoléon Charles, died in 1807 at the age of four, and Napoléon Louis became Prince Royal of Holland. As the second eldest nephew of Napoleon I, who then had no legitimate children, he was regarded as his uncle's likely eventual successor. He lost that presumptive status on 20 March 1811, when Napoleon's second wife, Marie Louise, gave birth to a son, Napoléon François Joseph Charles Bonaparte, styled the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and later Duke of Reichstadt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1809 Napoleon I appointed him Grand Duke of Berg, a position he held until 1813. His title also included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>. On 1 July 1810 Louis I of Holland abdicated in his favour, and for the nine days between his father's abdication and the fall of Holland to the advancing French army he reigned as Lodewijk II, King of Holland.</p><p>When Napoleon I was deposed in 1815 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Battle of Waterloo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">House of Bourbon</a> was restored to the French throne, Napoléon Louis fled into exile, although the Bonaparte family never abandoned hopes of restoring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-french-empire">Napoleonic Empire</a>. On 23 July 1826 he married his first cousin Charlotte, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-bonaparte">Joseph Bonaparte</a>, the eldest brother of Napoleon I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He and his brother Louis-Napoléon Bonaparte settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, where they embraced liberal politics and became involved with the Carbonari, who opposed Austrian domination of northern Italy. On 17 March 1831, while fleeing Italy during a crackdown on revolutionary activity by Papal and Austrian forces, Napoléon Louis died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> at Forlì. He is buried at Saint-Leu-la-Forêt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-france">Île-de-France</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> was eventually restored by his younger brother, who became Napoléon III in 1852.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Napoléon Louis Bonaparte” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Murat became King of Naples in 1808, Napoleon ruled Berg himself on behalf of his young nephew <b>Napoleon Louis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Forlì, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1821" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Napoléon Louis Bonaparte"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1821">What was here in 1821? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
